<speak>
<p>Witness. Field Season One. The closing synthesis: What the Counted Teach.</p>
<break time="800ms"/>
<p>A last field note.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>You made it. A porpoise, a parrot, a rhino, a wolf, a crow, a tree, a leopard, a salamander — you have met all eight now, and the letter's promise at the season's start has come due. It said the counted few, taken together, would have something to teach. Here is what they taught us, writing beside you.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>What the counted teach.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>Counting changes the counter. Somewhere in this season the numbers turned into names — Typhoon calling from a camera trap, a kākāpō roll call two hundred and thirty-six long, wolves known one by one in a North Carolina marsh. A statistic asks for your opinion; a name asks for your loyalty. When a species becomes individuals, the people counting stop being observers, and become the kind of people who swim a mother porpoise's sea at night, pulling nets. That transformation — in the counters, not the counted — is the engine of every recovery in this season.</p>
<p>The quietest lever is stopping. The season's biggest comeback needed no laboratory: the Amur leopard multiplied several times over when the killing, the burning, and the taking simply stopped. Chapter seven asked the question once, and the whole season answers it: before we ask what more we can do, the counted few ask what we could cease doing.</p>
<p>Rescue is slow, humble, and real. Eighteen axolotls with radio transmitters. Five crows on a mountain they had never seen. Chicks weighed nightly on an island at the bottom of the world. Comebacks are built one individual at a time — the same unit the losses came in. Nothing in this season was rescued at scale; everything that recovered, recovered by name.</p>
<p>The machines repeat, so the fixes travel. Two interludes traced the same machinery through story after story — the trade in parts, the uninvited we bring along. But the repairs repeat too. A refuge sealed by filters in Xochimilco rhymes with a peninsula cleared in Wellington, a quarantined canyon in Australia, a demand campaign in Vietnam. A lever proven in one chapter is already working in another. That is the practical hope of this season: nothing needs inventing twice.</p>
<p>And every story has someone who stayed. Rangers who walk Ujung Kulon. Farmers who build walls of volcanic rock beside their crops. A recovery team that knows every parrot's temperament. Trappers checking a backyard line in the rain. Not one of the eight is drifting toward survival on its own; each is being carried, shift by shift, by particular people. Hope, in this season, was never a feeling. It was a staff roster.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>What is known: all eight of the counted few persist as this season closes. Each has at least one lever demonstrably working. Each has people who stayed.</p>
<p>What is unknown: whether around ten vaquitas are enough to continue from. Whether the rhino's true count is fifty, or nearly a hundred. Whether the mosquitoes fall before the ʻalalā's window closes. Whether a megacity leaves water enough for a salamander. An honest season must say it plainly: the day may come when this edition holds fewer than eight living names. That is not a reason to look away. It is the reason witnessing exists.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The future. What you can carry.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>One. Keep one name. Of the eight, one stayed with you — you know which. Keep it. Every chapter's timeline carries a last-verified date, and this atlas will keep re-verifying; check on your name from time to time, the way you would any friend at a distance.</p>
<p>Two. Walk one door. This season holds more than a dozen doors, each one real, verified, and independent of us. Nobody walks through all of them. One is enough.</p>
<p>Three. Share the honest version. Every piece here has a share button, and the message it sends keeps the season's manners: counts stated, unknowns included, no comfort we cannot source. The truth traveled well for four hundred pages; it will survive a text message.</p>
<p>Four. Come back. Counting is a ritual, not an event — the counted few will not hold still. The numbers on the season's plate were true on the day they were verified, and the honest thing about a date is that it ages. Witnessing means returning.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>One question to carry with you. Eight names came with this season. Close the book, and say them back, from memory. However many return to you — that is your count, and it is where your witnessing stands tonight. It will grow.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>Every claim in this synthesis is made and sourced in the season's chapters. This narration uses a synthetic voice. Thank you for witnessing — and thank you for completing Field Season One.</p>
</speak>
